<p><img src="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/11/crate-header-rb.7542.jpg" width="800" height="226" style="margin:0 auto;clear:both;" class="header" /></p>
<p>My friend Eric Linder has been hard at work on a new app that, honestly, you have to check out. It has helped me remember so much of my music collection and discover new music that I love, and made it a pleasure to listen to in new ways. Since I started testing this app I have listened to at least four times as much music as I had been and I find myself constantly going back to it, as opposed to just playing the playlists I already know I like. I&rsquo;m going to let Eric tell you more about it.</p>


              
              <p><img src="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/11/crate-08-ipad-backroom-800.jpg"></p>
              
              <figcaption>The Back Room on iPad</figcaption>
            

<p>A favorite scene in the movie <em>Almost Famous</em> is when a young William Miller flips through the record albums that his sister gives him. As a kid, I loved similarly going through my dad&rsquo;s albums, reading the liner notes, and making connections between different bands and artists.</p>

<p>I wanted to recapture that feeling with <em>Crate.</em></p>

<p><strong>What&rsquo;s a crate?</strong></p>

<p>Crate has the standard features you&rsquo;d expect from an app like this: playlists, a queue, shuffle, and so on. But one of its newer ideas is what I call a crate.</p>


              
              <p><img src="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/11/crate-06-ipad-crates-400.jpg"></p>
              
              <figcaption>Crates on iPad</figcaption>
            
<p>If you&rsquo;re old enough to have gone to an actual music store, you may remember flipping through a bin or box of records until something caught your eye. In the app, a crate is a handful of about a dozen of your own albums that share something. Every day, Apple Intelligence suggests new ones, grouped by the same label, the same city, or even the same first name. Keep the ones you like, drop the rest, or make your own by picking up to twelve albums or describing them in a few words. You can also listen to a crate in interesting ways, like hearing three songs from each album before moving on to the next.</p>

<p>Crate turns your Apple Music library into a room full of records. A few of my favorite features:</p>


              
              <p><img src="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/11/crate-09-iphone-roadtrip-400.jpg"></p>
              
              <figcaption>A Road Trip on iPhone</figcaption>
            

<ul>
  <li><strong>Pick up where you left off, down to the second.</strong> Stop an album halfway through &ldquo;Deacon Blues&rdquo; and Crate keeps your place, for each of your last five albums, crates, and playlists. It follows you from iPhone to iPad to CarPlay to the Apple Watch.</li>
  <li><strong>The Clerk.</strong> Type what you want in plain words, like &ldquo;sunday morning soul from the 70s&rdquo; or &ldquo;Leonard Cohen songs sung by anybody but Leonard Cohen,&rdquo; and it builds a playlist only from songs you already own. It gives a reason for every pick, and says so plainly when it can&rsquo;t do something, instead of padding the list with near misses. It runs on Apple Intelligence, so your request stays on your device.</li>
  <li><strong>Liner notes for every album.</strong> Who produced it, who played on it, and room for your own note on the sleeve. Tap a producer or a session player and you see every record of yours they worked on. It&rsquo;s a little humbling to learn how much of your collection one drummer is quietly holding up.</li>
  <li><strong>The Friday Box.</strong> Every Friday, a cardboard box of new releases arrives, each one connected to records you already own through a label, a musician, or a hometown, with a sticker saying why it&rsquo;s there. You hear thirty seconds of every track, then keep it, set it aside, or send it back.</li>
  <li><strong>Wander.</strong> Start from any artist and see who their fans also listen to. Tap a name to hear thirty seconds, and that artist&rsquo;s neighbors spread out around them. It&rsquo;s laid out as a street of seven shops, each stocking its own kind of music: step to Miles Davis and you&rsquo;re in the jazz club. The artists you own light up, and when you find ones you like, keep them and Make a Crate from their albums.</li>
  <li><strong>Pop-Up Facts and Road Trips.</strong> While a song plays, a bubble appears every half minute with a story about how it was written or who played on it. And you can travel through your own records: one song from every year, or town to town on a map by where your artists were born.</li>
  <li><strong>Sort your shelves the way you&rsquo;d sort physical albums or CDs.</strong> Your library can be viewed as a wall of album covers, and on iPad and Mac you can zoom it from a wall of tiny covers all the way up to Big Sleeves. Sort tabs work like the dividers in a record bin (by year, genre, recently added, and more), with decade posters and genre-town banners marking each section, and filters you peel off like price stickers.</li>
  <li><strong>Mixtapes (Smartlists).</strong> Create a smart playlist that updates itself. They&rsquo;re easy and fun to make, and each one has its own look.</li>
</ul>

<p>The iPhone and iPad version of the app is already on the App Store, and the Mac app is in beta testing.</p>


              
              <p><img src="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/11/crate-10-iphone-widgets.jpg"></p>
              
              <figcaption>Album of the Day widget on an iPhone Home Screen</figcaption>
            

<p>The Mac app has some features unique to it, such as a mini player that sits on your desktop and an equalizer that can switch presets by genre as each song starts. Crate also runs as an MCP server on the Mac, so Claude or ChatGPT can search your library, play songs, rate them, and more.</p>

